<h3>The 3-pound rule nobody prints on the box</h3>

<p>Occupational-health guidance — the rules written for people who hold tools all day for a living — caps a one-handed tool at <strong>1.4 kg: about 3 pounds</strong>. Heavier than that, the guidance says, and the tool needs a counterbalance or a strap, because a human arm holding weight away from the body fatigues fast and keeps fatiguing faster.</p>

<p>Now weigh the blowers marketed as powerful. The EGO at rank 2: roughly <strong>10 pounds</strong> with its battery — a professional tester called it "one of the heaviest blowers of its kind." The DEWALT: about <strong>7 pounds</strong> ready to run. Gas backpacks: over 20. Every one of them is two to five times past the weight a hand is built to hold, and every one of them is why the job keeps getting abandoned in the middle.</p>

