<h3>The number that matters, and the two that don't</h3>

<p>This market runs on three numbers, and buyers have learned the hard way which ones lie. <strong>CFM</strong> — air volume — is the real currency for moving piles of leaves, and we printed every rival's figure on its card. <strong>Watts and RPM</strong> are marketing: they describe the motor, not the air, and shoppers on the deal forums openly mock listings built on them.</p>

<p>So here's our own awkward disclosure, made on purpose: the Wood Ranger's maker publishes motor figures — 1600W, up to 55,000 RPM — and no tube-measured CFM. We don't extend the benefit of the doubt on specs. <strong>We scored its Raw Airflow at the bottom of this entire set</strong>, you can see the short bar on its card, and it ranks #1 anyway — because a razor-focused, high-velocity stream you can aim with one hand finishes hard-surface jobs that raw volume figures were never about. The honest version of this category is simple: volume clears acreage, velocity clears pavement. Buy the number that matches your yard.</p>

