<p>Three tiers, honestly drawn. <strong>Hard surfaces and quick cleanups</strong> — patio, deck, driveway, garage floor, grass off the mower deck: this is 90% of what a homeowner's blower actually does, it's a five-minute task, and it's the Wood Ranger's home turf. <strong>Mixed dry leaves on a small lot:</strong> the Worx and DEWALT earn their weight here. <strong>Half an acre of wet November leaves:</strong> that's 600-plus CFM territory — the EGO, conceded without a fight.</p>

<p>And the two-tool household isn't a compromise, it's how blower owners already live: <em>"that's why I have 3 blowers. My 20V DeWalt will do the patio and 60 foot driveway… but when the leaves are abundant, wet &amp; heavy then it uses up the battery fast."</em> The big machine stays in the shed for the two weekends a year that need it. The light one lives by the door and gets grabbed forty times a season. At $69.99 complete, the grab-and-go slot is exactly what this tool is priced for.</p>

<p><a class="btn" href="${WR_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener">Check Today's Price — Complete Kit →</a></p>

<h3>The gas ritual, deleted</h3>

<p>If you came from gas, you already know the real reason you're here, and it isn't airflow. It's the ritual: <em>"I have a heck of a time getting the stupid thing started – the engine just won't 'catch.'"</em> The fuel mixing. The spring carburetor surgery. The fumes — one owner with lung trouble: <em>"the 2 stroke fumes would choke off my ability to breath."</em></p>

<p>None of that pain was ever the price of moving air. It was the price of a gasoline engine. Press the Wood Ranger's button and it's already running — brushless, no choke, no cord to yank, nothing to mix, nothing to winterize, and quiet enough that clearing the porch at 7am doesn't make you the neighborhood villain. The part of blowing leaves you hated is the part that's gone.</p>

<h3>What we'd tell you not to buy it for</h3>

